  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>How Concrete Bags Are Calculated (Exact US Yields &amp; Formulas)</h2>
    <p>
      Standard 4,000 PSI pre-blended concrete mix (ASTM C387, such as Quikrete #1101 or Sakrete High Strength) yields <strong>0.0075 cubic feet per dry pound</strong> once mixed with clean potable water. That locks in the three jobsite yield constants used across the US:
    </p>
    <div class="ac-formula">
Slab Volume (cu ft)    = Length (ft) × Width (ft) × (Thickness in inches ÷ 12)
Footing Volume (cu ft) = Linear Feet × (Width in inches ÷ 12) × (Depth in inches ÷ 12)
Cylinder Volume (cu ft)= π × ((Diameter in inches ÷ 2) ÷ 12)² × (Depth in inches ÷ 12) × Quantity

Adjusted Volume (cu ft)= Raw Volume (cu ft) × (1 + Waste% ÷ 100)
Cubic Yards (cu yd)    = Adjusted Volume (cu ft) ÷ 27

40 lb Bags (0.30 cu ft/bag) = ceil(Adjusted cu ft ÷ 0.30)  →  90 bags per cubic yard
60 lb Bags (0.45 cu ft/bag) = ceil(Adjusted cu ft ÷ 0.45)  →  60 bags per cubic yard
80 lb Bags (0.60 cu ft/bag) = ceil(Adjusted cu ft ÷ 0.60)  →  45 bags per cubic yard
    </div>
    <h3>Practical Contractor Field Rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Why Add 10% Waste?</strong> Excavated gravel subbases are rarely laser-flat. Just a ½-inch dip across a 10×12 ft slab adds 5 cubic feet (~8 extra 80 lb bags). Running short mid-pour creates a weak cold joint and forces an emergency hardware run.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry Weight vs. Pickup Payload:</strong> An 80 lb pallet has <strong>42 bags = 3,360 lbs</strong>, which exceeds the legal payload of most half-ton (1500/F-150) pickup trucks (~1,500–2,000 lbs). Split large bag runs into two trips or schedule flatbed delivery.</li>
      <li><strong>Do Not Over-Water the Mix:</strong> Adding extra water beyond manufacturer limits (~3 quarts per 80 lb bag) makes concrete easier to screed, but cuts cured compressive strength by 25–40% and increases surface scaling in freeze-thaw climates.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

